WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.930
Let's go to the phones at (617) 830-4750 a
lot of people eager to get on today. Let's

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:11.040
start with our caller from the eight oh six
area code. Who's calling today from eight

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.040
oh six

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:13.750
hello. How's it going?

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.169
Good. Who's this?

00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:16.390
My name's uh, Lamar.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:18.180
Hey Lamont. What's up?

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.180
Hey, I just had a quick question. It's a little
open ended. As you know, there's a lot of

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:28.850
democratic candidates in particular mind,
which is Bernie Sanders talking about increasing

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:34.210
minimum wage. Now I know that he and many
others have been trying to do this since the

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:41.579
nineties et cetera. I wanted to know even
after he leaves, what we have is a major problem

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:47.249
systemic, in my opinion, which is the cost
of inflation or the rate of inflation continues

00:00:47.249 --> 00:00:53.710
to change. I know that there's different things
like the fair Labor Standard Act or different

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:59.019
acts that were made. I just wanted to hear
your thoughts on do you believe that whoever

00:00:59.019 --> 00:01:03.949
the candidate is, whether it Bernie or Elizabeth
or whoever should set up an agency or give

00:01:03.949 --> 00:01:09.740
more power to an agency that's independent
of Congress to be able to naturally adjust

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:15.180
the minimum wage without having to go through
Congress with a great luck, a bunch of foolishness,

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:16.180
et cetera?

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:22.030
Yeah, I mean, so the answer is yes, the minimum
wage should be indexed to inflation, inflation

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:29.190
merely to keep up with inflation. The, the
more complicated element of it is that cost

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:35.311
of living is arguably, uh, more difficult
to pin down than national inflation. And that

00:01:35.311 --> 00:01:39.240
because the minimum wage is very regionalized,
what counts as a living wage in some parts

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:45.450
of the country is not in other parts of the
country. So there are some studies which suggest

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.460
that you should actually tie the minimum wage
to cost of living, which of course is very,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.110
very different in different parts of the country
and even within states. And that's where we

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.800
should be looking in po both politically and
logistically. That's much more difficult.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:01.500
Now let me say one other thing, Lamont. We
actually have it pretty good in the United

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:07.770
States as far as inflation in my birth country
of Argentina, the inflation is, I mean it's,

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:13.680
it's been cartoonish for 15 years. It's crazy
what's going on there. And in the u s fortunately,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.249
yes, there's a political obstacle to overcome
in terms of getting the minimum wage index

00:02:19.249 --> 00:02:25.209
to inflation, but at least our inflation is
very, very low compared to what's going on

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:29.900
in countries like Argentina. And it's not
nearly as big of a problem as it could be.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:34.599
Thank you. I really appreciate your thoughts.
Love you. So keep up the great work.

00:02:34.599 --> 00:02:36.890
All right. Thank you very much. I appreciate
hearing from you Lamont.

